  <h2>How does the calculation work?</h2>

  <p>
    Both sides reduce to one number: what reaches the owner’s account over a year. The
    arithmetic is not complicated, and it is worth doing on paper before signing anything.
  </p>

  <p><strong>Short-let, annual net:</strong></p>

  <ol>
    <li>Nightly rate × 365 × occupancy = gross booking revenue</li>
    <li>Less the management fee, at 15–25% of that gross</li>
    <li>Less cleaning, at the per-turnover charge × the number of stays</li>
    <li>Less the tourism dirham, per occupied night</li>
    <li>Less utilities, internet and consumables for the full year</li>
    <li>Less the DET permit, apportioned over its validity period</li>
    <li>Less replacement of linen, furnishing and appliances</li>
  </ol>

  <p><strong>Long-term tenancy, annual net:</strong></p>

  <ol>
    <li>Annual rent agreed in the contract</li>
    <li>Less any letting or management fee</li>
    <li>Less Ejari registration</li>
    <li>Less maintenance</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Both then have the service charge deducted, which is identical under either model and is
    therefore not what decides between them.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the real numbers?</h2>

  <p>
    The structure above is the mechanism; the inputs are what turn it into an answer, and they
    are specific and published rather than estimable. This page does not ship with invented
    figures in place of them:{' '}
    <NeedsSource what="Dubai short-let occupancy rates by community, tourism dirham per night by category, DET permit fee, typical cleaning charge per turnover, and comparable long-let rents" />
  </p>

  <p>
    What can be said now is which way each input pushes. Occupancy is the dominant term:
    everything else is roughly linear, but a unit at low occupancy pays its fixed costs out of
    a much smaller gross. That is why two identical apartments in the same tower can land on
    opposite sides of this decision.
  </p>

  <h2>What does the tourism dirham change?</h2>

  <p>
    It is charged per occupied night, so it scales with the thing you are trying to maximise.
    High occupancy raises the tourism dirham bill in step with revenue, which does not change
    the ranking but does flatten the advantage. The current rates by property category are set
    by the Department of Economy and Tourism and belong here as figures, above.
  </p>

  <h2>What do people get wrong?</h2>

  <p>
    Three things, consistently. They compare short-let <em>gross</em> against long-let
    <em>net</em>. They use a peak-season nightly rate as though it applied year-round. And they
    forget that a long-term tenancy takes almost none of their attention, which has a value
    even if it does not appear on either side of the calculation.
  </p>

  <p>
    A fourth, worth stating plainly: nobody should be projecting a return to you here. H1 2026
    residential transactions fell 13.6% year on year, and Q2 2026 saw both prices and rents
    decline. An operator forecasting a yield into that market is selling, not advising.
  </p>

  <p>
    What operators charge and what the fee does and does not cover is on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/">holiday home management in Dubai</a>, and the
    permit you cannot legally short-let without is on{' '}
    <a href="/holiday-home-management-dubai/permit/">the DET holiday home permit page</a>.
  </p>

